{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://kubaik.github.io/why-your-multi-agent-system-degraded-slowly/#faq",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I know if my multi-agent system’s degradation is from orchestration overhead?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Check three metrics: p99 latency, payload size, and broker queue depth. If p99 latency is rising but agent CPU and memory are flat, the problem is orchestration overhead. If payload size has grown by >50% in the last three months, that’s a strong signal. Use OpenTelemetry to trace message flow and identify where latency is added. A common failure mode is seeing 80% of latency in the orchestrator’s serialization and network layers, not in agent processing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I switch to gRPC instead of JSON-RPC?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Switching to gRPC won’t help unless you also address payload size growth and broker queueing. gRPC adds ~1-2ms of framing overhead vs. JSON-RPC’s ~0.5ms. The real win is reducing payload size with Protobuf and tuning NATS. gRPC is better for bidirectional streaming, but for request-reply orchestration, Protobuf over NATS is often simpler."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "My team uses Node.js for the orchestrator. Is this problem the same?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Node.js (v20 LTS) has similar issues with JSON payloads and GC pauses. The Node.js V8 engine’s GC pauses increase with large payloads, adding ~2-3ms to p99 latency. The fix is the same: reduce payload size, reuse connections, and monitor orchestration overhead with OpenTelemetry. Node.js’s event loop adds ~1ms of overhead per message, which compounds with fan-out."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "We’ve already tuned NATS and switched to Protobuf. Why is p99 still high?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Check for retry loops and connection pooling. If your orchestrator retries 5% of requests without adjusting timeouts, the retry overhead can add 250ms to p99 latency. Also, verify that your NATS connections are pooled—new TCP handshakes add ~0.5ms per request. Finally, check for GC pauses in your orchestrator language. Python 3.11’s GC pauses increase with payload size, even after switching to Protobuf."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much latency does a single retry add?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A single retry adds ~250ms to p99 latency in a system with 8 agents and 30ms timeouts. The retry itself takes 30ms, but the queueing pressure it creates adds another 220ms. Retries are a positive feedback loop—the more you retry, the worse latency gets. The fix is to increase timeouts or reduce the need for retries by fixing the root cause (payload size, broker queueing)."
      }
    }
  ]
}
